From e38228f033acb9fc3d96f3094c363d3d49f5e034 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 25 Nov 2026 01:10:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [Hades][dvbridge] A 3D LUT of the display as the last colour
 step of what the bridge maps

A display calibrated with a 3D LUT (Calman, ColourSpace, DisplayCAL, madVR)
had no way to use it here: Kodi's own colour management (CColorManager) is
of its desktop OpenGL renderer only, and the pictures that Hades maps to a
display are drawn by the bridge.

dvbridge_lut.c reads a LUT exactly as its file says, or refuses it with the
reason:
- .cube: LUT_3D_SIZE (2 to 256), TITLE, a domain and an input range of
  [0, 1]; a 1D LUT, another domain, or any other keyword is refused
  (libplacebo's pl_lut_parse_cube() passes unknown keywords over, reads a
  file with a 1D and a 3D size as one of them, scales the output by the
  domain and takes a first data line indented by a space for a header);
- .3dl (Lustre, Nuke): a uniform shaper, or Nuke's and Flame's (0, 64, ...,
  960, 1023), whose shorter last interval is kept by a shaper of two slopes
  in the shader; the output depth of Mesh, or the smallest that holds the
  values;
- madVR's .3dlut, as Kodi's CColorManager reads it (version 1, RGB, 16-bit
  output, uncompressed), with the range, primaries and transfer of its
  parameters (video levels where it says none, as madVR and Kodi take it).
Numbers are read in the C locale whatever the process's.

The table goes to libplacebo's pl_shader_custom_lut(): tetrahedral
interpolation from a 3D texture that sh_lut() makes RGBA32F on OpenGL ES 3
(the first float format that is not emulated), so the LUT gives the file's
values at its grid points.

dvbridge_render_set_lut(): the LUT is applied in the output hook, after the
tone mapping, the trims, the lift of the display's black and the hold of its
peak, and before a picture kept as 10-bit codes is quantized (once), in
dvbridge_render_hdr10() and dvbridge_render_plain() (not into the tunnel),
and in Low-Latency, whose bars stay at code 0 also where they are not
written first. Never for native Dolby Vision that the TV maps, whatever is
set. A render with it that fails is made again without it, and the
renderer says so (dvbridge_render_lut_failed()); the reference renderer of
the byte check takes it from the main one. Without a LUT the hooks and
their signatures are those before: every picture the same bytes.

Measured on Mesa's zink (tools/regress of LibreELEC Hades, stage lut3d of
dvbridge-render): every picture of the battery as its references with no
LUT; an identity LUT of 17, 33 or 65 points gives every 10-bit and 12-bit
code of the picture without it (the floats within 6e-8), mapped to SDR, to
a projector of 150 cd/m2 with and without its black, as it is, as 10-bit
codes and in Low-Latency; a known LUT in each format within 2e-7 of the
same LUT applied in float64 to the picture without it. On the software
renderer at 3840x2160 a whole render of a mapped picture took 10 to 25 %
longer with a LUT (46.1 against 52.4 ms mapped to SDR, 70.7 against 78.6 in
Low-Latency, on the quietest run), all of it in the pass that writes the
output: four reads of the table a pixel, no pass of its own. On a graphics
chip kodi.log gives the time of that pass.
